      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $20{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2}=12t-3{t}^{2},$ solve for $t.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's rewrite the polynomial equation in the form ${P}_{n}(t)=0.$ To do this, we subtract all the terms from the right-hand side so that the right-hand side becomes $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{t}^{3}+5{t}^{2} & =12t-3{t}^{2} \\\\ 20{t}^{3}+8{t}^{2} & =12t \\\\ 20{t}^{3}+8{t}^{2}-12t & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, notice that all of the terms of the polynomial have a common factor of $4t.$ We can factor out the $4t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{t}^{3}+8{t}^{2}-12t & =0 \\\\ 4t(5{t}^{2}+2t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $5{t}^{2}+2t-3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4t(5{t}^{2}+2t-3) & =0 \\\\ 4t(5t-3)(t+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by applying the zero product rule, the solutions are $t=0,$ $t=\\frac{3}{5},$ and $t=-1.$",
